\chapter*{Preface}
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Preface}

This book is a comprehensive treatise on the art and science of programming language development. It covers the full spectrum---from the mathematical foundations of logic, type theory, and category theory, through the design of formal semantics and the construction of compilers and interpreters, to the cutting edge of modern compiler architectures that blur the line between compile time and run time.

The approach is deliberately theoretical. We believe that a deep understanding of the mathematical structures underlying programming languages is not a luxury but a necessity. The languages we build, the compilers we write, and the analyses we perform are all manifestations of abstract mathematical ideas. To build them well, one must understand those ideas.

This book is not a cookbook. It is closer in spirit to Muchnick's \textit{Advanced Compiler Design and Implementation} than to a typical undergraduate textbook. We present the theory in depth, develop the mathematics carefully, and then show how it applies to real compiler and interpreter design. We do not shy away from abstraction; we embrace it. But we also ground every abstraction in concrete examples, pseudocode, and implementation sketches.

Parts I--XIV introduce the theory, core techniques, and terminology. Parts
XV--XXVII return to these topics through worked examples and case studies,
from language front ends and Lisp implementation through native code
generation and adaptive execution. Each chapter can be read on its own,
with cross-references for readers following a particular design problem.
The companion \emph{Modern Compiler Dossier} develops research proposals;
the final part of this book distinguishes those proposals from established
techniques and asks how to test them.
The companion's \emph{Tome Design Review} pairs its 45 chapters with
readings from this book and provides a worked design for guard failure and
version publication.

\paragraph{Reading the pseudocode.}
The walkthroughs in this edition pair algorithms with their assumptions
and a concrete trace. Read the assumptions before following the steps:
a dataflow worklist needs a convergence argument, an arithmetic
rewrite needs an overflow convention, and a runtime transition needs an
account of what state survives. Comments explain why a step is necessary
or what can go wrong if it is omitted. Use the trace as a first test of an
implementation, then try the boundary case discussed in the text. A passing
trace is evidence about that input, not a substitute for an invariant or
a proof.

